title: "rbGyanX: A research framework for radiobiological evaluation of radiotherapy plans (TCP/NTCP) from DICOM-RT"
version: "1.5.2"
# The date this version was released, not the date of the previous one. It said 2026-10-17 --
# v1.4.0's date -- beside version 1.5.0, which is the same class of stale metadata as the
# "1.3.0" string v1.4.0 shipped with: a field nothing asserted on. tests/test_version_consistency.py
# now checks it against the CHANGELOG entry for the declared version.
date-released: 2026-11-13
# Concept DOI -- ALL VERSIONS. It always resolves to the newest release, so it is never tied to
# one version and must not be labelled with one. Cite this for a general reference to the
# software; cite the version DOI of the release you actually ran when reproducibility matters
# (v1.5.0 is 10.5281/zenodo.23989624, v1.4.0 is 10.5281/zenodo.23447932). A citation harvested
# from this file should not pin a reader to a superseded release.
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21757163"
url: "https://github.com/kalyan2031990/rbGyanX"
repository-code: "https://github.com/kalyan2031990/rbGyanX"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - radiobiology
  - treatment plan evaluation
  - TCP
  - NTCP
  - radiotherapy
  - medical physics
abstract: "rbGyanX is a research framework for radiobiological evaluation of radiotherapy plans (TCP/NTCP) from DICOM-RT. It integrates physical dosimetry, radiobiological modelling and explainable machine learning for treatment plan evaluation in radiation oncology. For research use only. Not a medical device and not intended for clinical decision-making."
# This file cites THE SOFTWARE, and deliberately sets no `preferred-citation`.
#
# `preferred-citation` tells citation tools to cite something else *instead of* this software.
# Pointing it at a journal article would mean that anyone who used rbGyanX and reached for the
# citation file would credit a paper rather than the tool they ran — which misattributes the
# software and breaks the link between a result and the version that produced it. rbGyanX is
# released independently of any single publication.
#
# To credit a related publication, add it under `references:` (which is what that field is for)
# rather than under `preferred-citation:`, e.g.
#
# references:
#   - type: article
#     title: "<title>"
#     authors: [{family-names: Mondal, given-names: Kalyan}]
#     journal: "<journal>"
#     year: 2026
#     doi: "<article DOI>"
